// The random package: a seeded, repeatable pseudo-random generator, and what is
// drawn from it (integers in a range, floats in [0, 1), a shuffle, a choice,
// normal and exponential numbers). Written in Cone, with no C library beyond
// the OS's entropy call that seeding from the OS asks.
//
//   mod dice;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import random;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     mut rng = random.Rng.fromSeed(42u64);         // the same seed, the same numbers, anywhere
//     printInt(i64.from(rng.below(6u64)) + 1i64);   // a die: 1 to 6, no modulo bias
//     printStr(" ");
//     printInt(rng.rangeI64(-10i64, 10i64));        // -10 to 9
//     0i32;
//   }
//
// The generator is a VALUE: an 'Rng' holds its own state (four u64 and the
// spare half of a normal pair), with no global and no thread-local behind it.
// Make one per use, hand it to the function that needs numbers as '&mut Rng',
// give each thread its own ('jumped' makes streams that cannot overlap). The
// same 'Rng' value is never shared between threads.
//
// METHOD CHOICES. These are defaults a later design may revisit. Each says
// what was chosen and what was left out.
//
// - The generator is xoshiro256** 1.0 (Blackman and Vigna, 2018,
//   prng.di.unimi.it): 256 bits of state, period 2^256 - 1, 64 bits a step
//   from shifts, xors, rotates and two multiplies, no division and no float.
//   It passes the usual statistical batteries, is fast, and has a published
//   'jump' to 2^128 steps ahead, which makes independent streams for threads
//   or actors. Left out: PCG64 (its 128-bit multiply is two 64-bit multiplies
//   and a carry here, and it has no equally simple jump), and a generator
//   fit for cryptography: xoshiro256** is NOT one. Do not draw keys, tokens
//   or session ids from it, even seeded from the OS.
// - Seeding from a u64 ('fromSeed') expands the seed with SplitMix64, as the
//   xoshiro authors direct: the first four outputs of SplitMix64 started at
//   the seed are the four state words. Any seed, 0 included, gives a good
//   state. 'fromState' takes the four words as they are (an all-zero state,
//   which xoshiro cannot leave, is replaced by 'fromSeed(0)''s).
// - Seeding from the OS ('fromEntropy') draws all 256 bits of the state from
//   the OS's generator, the one conestd seeds its hashers from (packages/
//   conestd/hash.cone): on Windows the C runtime's 'rand_s', elsewhere
//   'getentropy'. It is not core's 'processSeed', which is one number the
//   same for the whole process, so two generators seeded from it would be
//   twins. The answer is an Option, None if the OS refuses (it does not in
//   practice); 'fromEntropyOr' takes a seed to fall back to. The two externs
//   are declared here, as conestd declares them: no C code is added.
// - An integer in [0, n) is Lemire's multiply-shift with rejection ("Fast
//   Random Integer Generation in an Interval", ACM TOMACS 2019): the high
//   64 bits of a 64-bit draw times n, redrawn in the rare case the low half
//   falls under 2^64 mod n. It is exactly uniform (no modulo bias) and
//   divides only on that rare path. The 64 x 64 -> 128 bit product is built
//   from four 32 x 32 products, in integers. Ranges are half-open
//   ('rangeU64', 'rangeI64') or closed ('closedU64', 'closedI64', which can
//   span the whole type); an empty or reversed range answers its low end.
// - A float in [0, 1) takes the top 53 bits of a draw and multiplies by 2^-53
//   ('unit', f64) or the top 24 bits and 2^-24 ('unitF32', f32): every value
//   is a multiple of 2^-53 (2^-24) and exactly as likely, 1.0 never comes, and
//   the conversion is exact. Left out: floats that reach the finest
//   representable values near 0 (the "full precision" kind).
// - 'shuffle' is the Fisher-Yates shuffle in Durstenfeld's form: from the
//   last place down, swap it with a place drawn from [0, i], each by 'below'.
//   Every order is equally likely. 'permutation(n)' is the shuffled list
//   0, 1, ..., n - 1, to shuffle an array or anything else by index. 'choice'
//   borrows one element of a list. Left out: sampling without replacement
//   and weighted choice.
// - 'normal' is the Marsaglia polar method (two draws make a point in the
//   square, kept if inside the unit circle, and the pair of normals the point
//   gives are one returned and one kept for the next call). Left out: the
//   ziggurat (it needs tables, and a table here is a global; globals hold) and
//   Box-Muller (a sine and a cosine). 'exponential' is -ln(1 - u) / rate, by
//   inversion.
// - Same bits on every platform. The integer path (every integer, the shuffle,
//   the choice, the floats from 'unit') is integer arithmetic and one exact
//   conversion and multiply, so it gives the same bits wherever it runs,
//   and the tests pin it against the reference implementations' published
//   numbers. 'normal' and 'exponential' need a logarithm and a square root.
//   The square root is IEEE's, correctly rounded everywhere. The logarithm
//   is NOT libm's 'log' (whose last bits differ between C libraries): it is
//   written here from additions, multiplications and divisions, in one fixed
//   order ('lnPositive', below), which are exact IEEE operations. Those give
//   the same bits on every machine as long as the compiler neither fuses a
//   multiply with an add nor reassociates, which conec does not (noise's
//   README measured it: separate fmul and fadd, no fast-math, generic x86-64
//   CPU with no FMA). It has been measured on Windows only; Linux is
//   expected to agree and is not yet tested. The logarithm is within a few
//   units in the last place of the true one, which a sample does not notice.
// - Several things this package does not do: 'noise' (the procedural
//   package) hashes with its own 'pcg' functions, a hash of coordinates and
//   not a stream, and 'audiomixer' keeps a private 32-bit PCG 'Rng' for its
//   noise generators. Neither is migrated; both stay as they are.
// - No global and no thread-local: the whole generator is the value.

mod random;

import collections use List;

// -------- the OS's entropy --------

extern {
  // The C runtime's 32 random bits from the OS: 0 if it drew them, else an error
  fn @c("rand_s") randS(value: *u32) i32;

  // Fills the 'len' bytes at 'buf' (at most 256) with the kernel's random bytes:
  // 0 if it did, else -1
  fn @c("getentropy") getEntropy(buf: *u8, len: usize) i32;
}

// 64 random bits from the OS, or None if it refused
fn osWord() Option[u64] {
  var low = 0u32;
  var high = 0u32;
  var bits = 0u64;
  if isWindows() {
    if randS(&mut low as *u32) != 0 || randS(&mut high as *u32) != 0 { return None; }
    bits = (u64.from(high) << 32u64) | u64.from(low);
  }
  else {
    if getEntropy(&mut bits as *u8, 8usize) != 0 { return None; }
  }
  Some[u64](bits)
}

// -------- SplitMix64 --------

// One step of SplitMix64 (Steele, Lea and Flood 2014; Vigna's splitmix64.c):
// the state moves on by an odd constant and the new state is scrambled. It is
// what 'fromSeed' expands a seed with, and is public for a caller who wants a
// generator of one u64 of state
pub fn splitMix64(state: &mut u64) u64 {
  *state = *state + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15u64;
  var z = *state;
  z = (z ^ (z >> 30u64)) * 0xbf58476d1ce4e5b9u64;
  z = (z ^ (z >> 27u64)) * 0x94d049bb133111ebu64;
  z ^ (z >> 31u64)
}

// -------- arithmetic the draws use --------

// The 128-bit product of two u64, as (high 64 bits, low 64 bits), from four
// 32 x 32 products
fn mulWide(a: u64, b: u64) (u64, u64) {
  let mask = 0xFFFFFFFFu64;
  let a0 = a & mask;
  let a1 = a >> 32u64;
  let b0 = b & mask;
  let b1 = b >> 32u64;
  let p00 = a0 * b0;
  let p01 = a0 * b1;
  let p10 = a1 * b0;
  let p11 = a1 * b1;
  // The middle column: its carry into the high half is at most 2
  let mid = (p00 >> 32u64) + (p01 & mask) + (p10 & mask);
  let high = p11 + (p01 >> 32u64) + (p10 >> 32u64) + (mid >> 32u64);
  (high, a * b)
}

// The natural logarithm of a positive, normal f64 (not a denormal, infinity or
// NaN: the draws never give one). x = m * 2^e with m in [sqrt(2)/2, sqrt(2)];
// ln m = 2 atanh s = 2 (s + s^3/3 + s^5/5 + ...) with s = (m - 1) / (m + 1),
// no more than 0.1716, so terms to s^21 leave less than 2^-60 of error. Only
// additions, multiplications and divisions, in this order, so every machine
// gets the same bits. The result is within a few ulp of the true logarithm.
fn lnPositive(x: f64) f64 {
  let bits = x as u64;
  var e = i64.from((bits >> 52u64) & 0x7FFu64) - 1023i64;
  var m = ((bits & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFu64) | 0x3FF0000000000000u64) as f64;
  if m > 1.4142135623730951 {
    m = m * 0.5;
    e = e + 1i64;
  }
  let s = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0);
  let t = s * s;
  var p = 1.0 / 21.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 19.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 17.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 15.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 13.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 11.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 9.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 7.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 5.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0 / 3.0;
  p = p * t + 1.0;
  f64.from(e) * 0.6931471805599453 + 2.0 * s * p
}

// The natural logarithm 'lnPositive' computes, for a test to compare with the
// C library's
pub fn naturalLog(x: f64) f64 { lnPositive(x) }

// -------- the generator --------

// A xoshiro256** generator. Its fields are its whole state, so copying the
// value (with 'copyOf') copies the stream.
pub struct Rng {
  s0: u64;
  s1: u64;
  s2: u64;
  s3: u64;
  // The second number of a normal pair, kept for the next 'normal' call
  spare: f64;
  hasSpare: bool;

  // The generator 'seed' names, the seed expanded by SplitMix64 into four
  // state words. The same seed gives the same numbers on every platform
  pub fn fromSeed(seed: u64) Rng {
    var sm = seed;
    let a = splitMix64(&mut sm);
    let b = splitMix64(&mut sm);
    let c = splitMix64(&mut sm);
    let d = splitMix64(&mut sm);
    Rng(a, b, c, d, 0.0, false)
  }

  // The generator with exactly these four state words. xoshiro cannot leave
  // the all-zero state, so that one gives 'fromSeed(0)''s instead
  pub fn fromState(s0: u64, s1: u64, s2: u64, s3: u64) Rng {
    if (s0 | s1 | s2 | s3) == 0u64 { return Rng.fromSeed(0u64); }
    Rng(s0, s1, s2, s3, 0.0, false)
  }

  // A generator whose 256 bits of state come from the OS, or None if the OS
  // would not give them
  pub fn fromEntropy() Option[Rng] {
    var words = 0usize;
    var w0 = 0u64;
    var w1 = 0u64;
    var w2 = 0u64;
    var w3 = 0u64;
    match osWord() { case let Some(v) { w0 = v; } case is None { return None; } }
    match osWord() { case let Some(v) { w1 = v; } case is None { return None; } }
    match osWord() { case let Some(v) { w2 = v; } case is None { return None; } }
    match osWord() { case let Some(v) { w3 = v; } case is None { return None; } }
    Some[Rng](Rng.fromState(w0, w1, w2, w3))
  }

  // 'fromEntropy', or 'fromSeed(seed)' if the OS gave nothing
  pub fn fromEntropyOr(seed: u64) Rng {
    match Rng.fromEntropy() {
      case var Some(r) { return r; }
      case is None { return Rng.fromSeed(seed); }
    }
    Rng.fromSeed(seed)
  }

  // The four state words, to save a generator and rebuild it with 'fromState'
  // (the spare half of a normal pair is not in them)
  pub fn state(self: &) (u64, u64, u64, u64) { (s0, s1, s2, s3) }

  // A generator in exactly this one's place, which goes on to give the
  // numbers this one will
  pub fn copyOf(self: &) Rng { Rng(s0, s1, s2, s3, spare, hasSpare) }

  // The next 64 random bits
  pub fn next(self: &mut) u64 {
    let result = (s1 * 5u64).rotateLeft(7u32) * 9u64;
    let t = s1 << 17u64;
    s2 = s2 ^ s0;
    s3 = s3 ^ s1;
    s1 = s1 ^ s2;
    s0 = s0 ^ s3;
    s2 = s2 ^ t;
    s3 = s3.rotateLeft(45u32);
    result
  }

  // The next 32 random bits: the high half of a draw
  pub fn nextU32(self: &mut) u32 { u32.from(self.next() >> 32u64) }

  // A fair coin
  pub fn nextBool(self: &mut) bool { (self.next() >> 63u64) == 1u64 }

  // True with probability p (never for p <= 0, always for p >= 1)
  pub fn chance(self: &mut, p: f64) bool { self.unit() < p }

  // Advance 2^128 steps at once, the reference 'jump': 2^128 non-overlapping
  // streams of 2^128 draws each, one per thread or actor
  pub fn jump(self: &mut) {
    var a = 0u64;
    var b = 0u64;
    var c = 0u64;
    var d = 0u64;
    var word = 0usize;
    while word < 4usize {
      let poly = jumpWord(word);
      var bit = 0u64;
      while bit < 64u64 {
        if (poly & (1u64 << bit)) != 0u64 {
          a = a ^ s0;
          b = b ^ s1;
          c = c ^ s2;
          d = d ^ s3;
        }
        self.next();
        bit = bit + 1u64;
      }
      word = word + 1usize;
    }
    s0 = a;
    s1 = b;
    s2 = c;
    s3 = d;
    hasSpare = false;
  }

  // A generator at this one's place, while this one jumps 2^128 steps ahead:
  // call it again for the next stream
  pub fn jumped(self: &mut) Rng {
    let child = self.copyOf();
    self.jump();
    child
  }

  // -------- integers --------

  // An integer in [0, n), every one equally likely (Lemire's method). 0 if n is 0
  pub fn below(self: &mut, n: u64) u64 {
    if n == 0u64 { return 0u64; }
    var m = mulWide(self.next(), n);
    if m.1 < n {
      // 2^64 mod n: the low halves under it come from one value too many
      let threshold = (0u64 - n) % n;
      while m.1 < threshold {
        m = mulWide(self.next(), n);
      }
    }
    m.0
  }

  // An index in [0, len) (0 for an empty length: check it first)
  pub fn index(self: &mut, len: usize) usize { usize.from(self.below(u64.from(len))) }

  // An integer in [lo, hi): lo if the range is empty
  pub fn rangeU64(self: &mut, lo: u64, hi: u64) u64 {
    if hi <= lo { return lo; }
    lo + self.below(hi - lo)
  }

  // An integer in [lo, hi]: lo if hi is under it; the whole type if lo is 0 and
  // hi the largest
  pub fn closedU64(self: &mut, lo: u64, hi: u64) u64 {
    if hi < lo { return lo; }
    let span = hi - lo + 1u64;
    if span == 0u64 { return self.next(); }
    lo + self.below(span)
  }

  // An integer in [lo, hi): lo if the range is empty
  pub fn rangeI64(self: &mut, lo: i64, hi: i64) i64 {
    if hi <= lo { return lo; }
    ((lo as u64) + self.below((hi as u64) - (lo as u64))) as i64
  }

  // An integer in [lo, hi]: lo if hi is under it; the whole type if the ends
  // are its ends
  pub fn closedI64(self: &mut, lo: i64, hi: i64) i64 {
    if hi < lo { return lo; }
    let span = (hi as u64) - (lo as u64) + 1u64;
    if span == 0u64 { return self.next() as i64; }
    ((lo as u64) + self.below(span)) as i64
  }

  // -------- floats --------

  // A float in [0, 1): a multiple of 2^-53, the top 53 bits of a draw
  pub fn unit(self: &mut) f64 {
    f64.from(self.next() >> 11u64) * (1.0 / 9007199254740992.0)
  }

  // A float in [0, 1): a multiple of 2^-24, the top 24 bits of a draw
  pub fn unitF32(self: &mut) f32 {
    f32.from(self.next() >> 40u64) * (1.0 / 16777216.0)
  }

  // A float in [lo, hi) (lo + (hi - lo) * unit: the top can round up to hi
  // when the ends are far from the same size)
  pub fn rangeF64(self: &mut, lo: f64, hi: f64) f64 { lo + (hi - lo) * self.unit() }

  // -------- lists --------

  // The list's values in a random order (Fisher-Yates), every order equally
  // likely
  pub fn shuffle[T](self: &mut, items: &mut List[T]) {
    var i = items.len();
    while i > 1usize {
      i = i - 1usize;
      let j = self.index(i + 1usize);
      items.swap(i, j);
    }
  }

  // The shuffled list 0, 1, ..., n - 1
  pub fn permutation(self: &mut, n: usize) List[usize] {
    var order = List[usize]();
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < n {
      order.push(i);
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.shuffle(&mut order);
    order
  }

  // The index of a random element of a list of this length: None if it is empty
  pub fn choiceIndex(self: &mut, len: usize) Option[usize] {
    if len == 0usize { return None; }
    Some[usize](self.index(len))
  }

  // A borrow of a random element of the list: None if it is empty
  pub fn choice[T](self: &mut, items: &'a List[T]) Option[&'a T] {
    if items.len() == 0usize { return None; }
    items.get(self.index(items.len()))
  }

  // -------- normal and exponential --------

  // A normal number, mean 0 and standard deviation 1 (Marsaglia's polar
  // method): the pair a draw gives is one now and one on the next call
  pub fn normal(self: &mut) f64 {
    if hasSpare {
      hasSpare = false;
      return spare;
    }
    var x = 0.0;
    var y = 0.0;
    var q = 0.0;
    while true {
      x = self.unit() * 2.0 - 1.0;
      y = self.unit() * 2.0 - 1.0;
      q = x * x + y * y;
      if q < 1.0 && q != 0.0 { break; }
    }
    let factor = (-2.0 * lnPositive(q) / q).sqrt();
    spare = y * factor;
    hasSpare = true;
    x * factor
  }

  // A normal number with this mean and standard deviation
  pub fn normalWith(self: &mut, mean: f64, deviation: f64) f64 { mean + deviation * self.normal() }

  // An exponential number of rate 1 (mean 1), by inversion: -ln(1 - u)
  pub fn exponential(self: &mut) f64 {
    0.0 - lnPositive(1.0 - self.unit())
  }

  // An exponential number of this rate (mean 1 / rate)
  pub fn exponentialWith(self: &mut, rate: f64) f64 { self.exponential() / rate }
}

// The four words of xoshiro256**'s 'jump' polynomial (the reference's JUMP)
fn jumpWord(i: usize) u64 {
  if i == 0usize { return 0x180ec6d33cfd0abau64; }
  if i == 1usize { return 0xd5a61266f0c9392cu64; }
  if i == 2usize { return 0xa9582618e03fc9aau64; }
  0x39abdc4529b1661cu64
}
